e=\"width: ${d.percent || 100}%;\"></div>\n1513:             </div>\n1514:             <div class=\"drive-meta\">${d.free_str} free of ${d.total_str}</div>\n1515:           </div>\n1516:         `;\n1517:         container.appendChild(card);\n1518:       });\n1519:     } catch (e) {\n1520:       console.error(e);\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":11090,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-26T14:30:47Z","thinking":"Drive space data is retrieved and confirmed to be accurate for both drives C: and D:. This confirms the data presentation aligns with the underlying disk statistics. The focus shifts towards examining the bottom navigation bar and identifying the removal of an extraneous button.\n\n\nThe structure and styling of the bottom navigation bar are being examined, focusing on its HTML implementation and associated CSS. This involves verifying the positioning as fixed at the bottom of the screen. The existing code for the bottom navigation bar is reviewed to ensure correct implementation of buttons and icons.\n\n\nThe CSS code defining the bottom navigation bar's visual properties, including positioning, sizing, background, and button styling, is being analyzed. The `.bottom-nav-item` class defines the individual button attributes, and the active state is also reviewed. Further investigation focuses on understanding how the `.bottom-cpu-icon-wrap` class relates to a specific icon within the navigation bar.\n\n\nThe `body` element's bottom margin is being adjusted to ensure the content area has adequate space.  The content areas now have bottom padding to prevent content overlap with the bottom navigation bar. The \"Windows 11 Home\" button is removed from the window title bar as specified. The mobile top bar HTML is being streamlined for improved compactness, focusing on the mobile menu button and simplified home buttons.\n\n\nJavaScript functions for the bottom navigation bar are being reviewed, specifically `handleBottomBack`, `handleBottomHome`, `handleBottomMonitor`, and `updateBottomNav`. The logic for activating and deactivating buttons based on the `activeTab` and `currentPath` is examined, including the opacity changes of the back button. The `fetchStats()` function is also examined for updating the CPU badge within the navigation bar, including setting the text and color.\n\n\nThe CPU monitoring graph's erratic behavior is analyzed, starting with understanding the one-minute timeframe. Historical data points within that timeframe are considered static, not subject to real-time fluctuations. Only the current second's data requires updating, with a new point appended and the oldest removed. The underlying charting library's default animation setting is identified as a likely cause for the jumping effect.\n\n\nThe graph's erratic behavior is caused by three issues: excessive animation of each data point, a floating Y-axis scale, and complete dataset replacement every few seconds, leading to a complete redraw. To fix this, the Y-axis must be fixed by specifying hard `min` and `max` values and disabling all chart animations.\n\n\nTo achieve a smooth real-time CPU graph, all animations within the charting library must be disabled, and updates should use a non-animated update strategy. The graph will be updated by adding a new data point every second, replacing the oldest data point to maintain a one-minute history, and only updating the current data point value if the epoch time matches. The latest data point should have a visual distinction, such as a larger radius.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"/home/azureuser/IrakIroan/azure_file_explorer/static/index.html","EndLine":1915,"StartLine":1780,"toolAction":"Viewing chart methods in index.html","toolSummary":"Inspect chart rendering"}}]}
